[section:multivariate_roots Multivariate Newton and Halley]

[import ../../example/multivariate_roots_ellipse.cpp]

[h4 Synopsis]

``
#include <boost/math/tools/multivariate_roots.hpp>
``

   namespace boost { namespace math { namespace tools {

   template <class F, class Container>
   Container multivariate_newton(F f, Container x, int digits);

   template <class F, class Container>
   Container multivariate_newton(F f, Container x, int digits, std::uintmax_t& max_iter);

   template <class F, class Container>
   Container multivariate_halley(F f, Container x, int digits);

   template <class F, class Container>
   Container multivariate_halley(F f, Container x, int digits, std::uintmax_t& max_iter);

   }}} // namespaces boost::math::tools

[h4 Description]

These functions find a root of a system of /n/ equations in /n/ unknowns, [*F](/x/) = 0 with /x/ in ℝ[super /n/],
starting from the guess `x`.
They are the multivariate analogues of `newton_raphson_iterate` and `halley_iterate` of __root_finding_with_derivatives.

* `multivariate_newton` solves /J/(/x/)/a/ = −[*F](/x/) for the Newton step /a/, and converges quadratically.
* `multivariate_halley` is the multivariate Halley method of Cuyt, which converges cubically.
With the Newton step /a/ and /b/ = /J/[super −1][*F]″(/x/)\[/a/, /a/\], it updates each component as

[expression /x/[sub /i/] ← /x/[sub /i/] + /a/[sub /i/][super 2] \/ (/a/[sub /i/] + /b/[sub /i/]\/2).]

This is the abstract Padé approximant of order (1, 1) to the inverse of [*F], with the vector operations taken componentwise:
when /n/ = 1 it is Halley's method.
One LU factorization of /J/ serves both linear solves, and the method needs [*F]″ only along the direction /a/,
so the /n/[super 3] entries of the second derivative tensor are never formed.

[variablelist Parameters
[[F f] [A callable that takes a `Container` /x/ and returns a pair or tuple.
For Newton it holds [*F](/x/) and the Jacobian /J/(/x/), /J/[sub /ij/] = ∂/F/[sub /i/]\/∂/x/[sub /j/].
For Halley it holds a third element, a callable `d2` with `d2(v)` = [*F]″(/x/)\[/v/, /v/\],
the second derivative of /t/ ↦ [*F](/x/ + /t v/) at /t/ = 0; Newton ignores a third element, so one callable serves both.
[*F](/x/) and `d2(v)` may be any containers with `size()` and `operator[]`.
/J/ may be a container of rows (`std::vector<std::vector<Real>>`, `std::array<std::array<Real, N>, N>`, ...),
or a row-major array of /n/[super 2] entries (`std::vector<Real>`, `std::array<Real, N*N>`, ...).]]
[[Container x] [The initial guess, a `std::vector<Real>`, `std::array<Real, N>`, or similar random access container
of a floating point type, which may be a multiprecision type.]]
[[int digits] [The desired number of binary digits of precision.]]
[[uintmax_t& max_iter] [An optional maximum number of iterations; on exit, the number of iterations performed.
It is the number of factorizations of /J/, and does not count the line search's evaluations of `f`.]]
]

The iteration stops when the Newton step /a/ satisfies ‖/a/‖[sub ∞] ≤ 2[super 1−digits] ‖/x/‖[sub ∞]
(the step is then taken), when [*F](/x/) = 0 exactly, or when `max_iter` iterations have been performed.
It also stops when steps below √ε‖/x/‖[sub ∞] no longer shrink at all, since they are then rounding noise;
at a multiple root, where the steps shrink by a constant factor, the iteration goes on.
As for the scalar functions, the tolerance is relative to /x/, so a root at the origin is approached until
[*F] underflows to zero.

[h4 Globalization]

Far from a root a full Newton step can increase ‖[*F]‖, so both functions minimize ½‖[*F]‖[super 2] along their steps.
Newton backtracks along /a/ until the Armijo condition ½‖[*F](/x/ + /t a/)‖[super 2] ≤ (1 − 2×10[super −4] /t/)½‖[*F](/x/)‖[super 2] holds,
choosing each new /t/ from the quadratic through ½‖[*F]‖[super 2] and its slope at /t/ = 0, kept in \[/t/\/10, /t/\/2\].
Halley tries its full step first, and if that does not decrease ‖[*F]‖ it takes the backtracking Newton step instead.
A trial point where [*F] is not finite is treated as an increase, so `f` may return infinities or NaNs outside its domain.

The componentwise division can blow up where /a/[sub /i/] + /b/[sub /i/]\/2 is near zero.
Cuyt and Rall take 0\/0 as 0 (the Halley component is zero when the Newton component is).
In addition, where /q/ = 1 + /b/[sub /i/]\/(2/a/[sub /i/]) < 1\/2, so that the Halley component points against the Newton
component or is more than twice as long, the Newton component /a/[sub /i/] is used in its place.
Near a root /b/ = /O/(‖/a/‖[super 2]) and /q/ → 1, so this does not affect the cubic convergence.

[h4 Errors]

An __evaluation_error is raised through the default policy (and so is thrown by default) when

* the Jacobian is singular: a pivot of its LU factorization with partial pivoting is not above /n/ε times the largest
entry of its column,
* no step along the Newton direction decreases ‖[*F]‖, as happens near a local minimum of ‖[*F]‖ that is not a root
(where /J/ is singular) or when /J/ is wrong,
* [*F] is not finite at the initial guess, or [*F], /J/ or `d2(v)` has the wrong size.

A domain error is raised when `x` is empty.

When no step decreases ‖[*F]‖ but the Newton step is below ε[super 1\/3]‖/x/‖[sub ∞], `x` is returned without an error:
at a local minimum of ‖[*F]‖ that is not a root, /J/ is singular and the Newton step large or undefined, whereas a step
this small is the rounding noise of an accurate /x/, about cond(/J/)ε‖/x/‖, for cond(/J/) up to ε[super −2\/3]
(10[super 10] in double precision).

With a policy that does not throw, every component of the result is the value returned by the error handler, NaN by default.

[h4 Supplying the derivatives]

[*F]″(/x/)\[/v/, /v/\] is the second derivative of the univariate function /t/ ↦ [*F](/x/ + /t v/) at /t/ = 0,
so a single second order forward-mode pass of [link math_toolkit.autodiff autodiff] in the one variable /t/ computes it, for any /n/.
The example [@../../example/multivariate_roots_ellipse.cpp multivariate_roots_ellipse.cpp] finds the point of an ellipse
closest to a point /q/: at the closest point /p/, /p/ is on the ellipse and /p/ − /q/ is along the normal:

[multivariate_roots_ellipse_system]

The Jacobian is built one column at a time by first order passes along the coordinate directions, and `d2` is a
second order pass along `v`, computed only when the solver asks for it:

[multivariate_roots_autodiff]

[multivariate_roots_ellipse_solve]

The closure `d2` may capture whatever it needs from the evaluation at /x/; for the Rosenbrock gradient system
∇((1 − /x/)[super 2] + 100(/y/ − /x/[super 2])[super 2]) = 0, written out by hand,

   auto f = [](std::vector<double> const& x) {
      double u = x[0], w = x[1];
      std::vector<double> F{-2 * (1 - u) - 400 * u * (w - u * u), 200 * (w - u * u)};
      std::vector<std::vector<double>> J{{2 - 400 * (w - u * u) + 800 * u * u, -400 * u}, {-400 * u, 200.0}};
      auto d2 = [u](std::vector<double> const& v) {
         return std::vector<double>{-800 * v[0] * v[1] + 2400 * u * v[0] * v[0], -400 * v[0] * v[0]};
      };
      return std::make_tuple(F, J, d2);
   };
   std::uintmax_t iterations = 1000;
   auto x = boost::math::tools::multivariate_halley(f, std::vector<double>{-1.2, 1.0}, 53, iterations);

[h4 Performance and caveats]

A Halley iteration costs one more triangular solve and one evaluation of `d2` than a Newton iteration, and saves
about a third of the iterations near the root; which is faster overall depends on the cost of `f` and `d2`
relative to the /O/(/n/[super 3]) factorization.
Far from the root Halley's steps follow curved valleys better: from the classic start (−1.2, 1), Newton with the
line search needs 253 iterations on the Rosenbrock gradient system, as its straight steps creep along the valley,
and Halley needs 68.

Newton's method is invariant under affine changes of the variables /x/ ↦ /Ax/; the multivariate Halley method is not,
because its update divides componentwise. It is invariant under scaling of the individual variables, and, like Newton,
under any invertible linear change of the equations [*F] ↦ /B/[*F].

The LU factorization is dense; for large sparse systems, solve with a sparse factorization in a loop of your own.
The line search guarantees that ‖[*F]‖ decreases, not that the root found is the one nearest the guess.

[h4 References]

* A. Cuyt and P. Van der Cruyssen, ['Abstract Padé approximants for the solution of a system of nonlinear equations],
Computers & Mathematics with Applications 9 (1983) 617–624.
* A. Cuyt, ['Numerical stability of the Halley-iteration for the solution of a system of nonlinear equations],
Mathematics of Computation 38 (1982) 171–179.
* A. A. M. Cuyt and L. B. Rall, ['Computational implementation of the multivariate Halley method for solving
nonlinear systems of equations], ACM Transactions on Mathematical Software 11 (1985) 20–36.
* J. E. Dennis and R. B. Schnabel, ['Numerical Methods for Unconstrained Optimization and Nonlinear Equations],
SIAM (1996), section 6.3.

